issues.body:
### Problem

`replaceOption` does not work when replacing an `AbortSignal` on an extended instance:

```ts
import ky, {replaceOption} from 'ky';

const baseController = new AbortController();
const extendedController = new AbortController();

const base = ky.create({
  signal: baseController.signal,
});

const extended = base.extend({
  signal: replaceOption(extendedController.signal),
});

// Aborting the base controller still aborts the extended instance!
baseController.abort();
await extended('https://example.com'); // Throws AbortError (unexpected)
```

Similarly, passing `{signal: undefined}` or `{signal: null}` in `.extend()` does not remove parent signals from being combined via `AbortSignal.any()`.

### Cause

In `source/utils/merge.ts` (`deepMergeInternal`):
1. The `if (key === 'signal' && value instanceof globalThis.AbortSignal)` check occurs **before** `const replaceState = getReplaceState(value)`.
2. When wrapping a signal in `replaceOption(signal)`, `value` is a symbol-tagged object and fails the `instanceof AbortSignal` check.
3. As a result, the parent signal remains in the `signals` array and gets merged via `AbortSignal.any()` at the end of the merge function, overriding the replaced value.

### Solution

Unwrap `replaceState` before the `key === 'signal'` branch and handle `isReplace` / `undefined` / `null` to reset the accumulated `signals` array.

Full fix and tests are available on fork branch: https://github.com/koding88/ky/commit/99b41dd51a37c95e1e07b8b7d41f39cb70503e4d
